                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_474">St Albans,
                        Hertfordshire. Benedictine abbey of St Alban. </orgName><p>'H[unc] l[ibrum
                        dedit] D[ominus] R[icardus de] B[ury] E[piscopus] D[une]l[mensis Domini
                        sancti Albani], fol. iii recto at top, erased and illegible except for the
                        tops of the ascenders, which correspond exactly in script and position with
                        those in Laud Misc. 363.</p><p>Fol. 1r at top:
                        'H[............................] anathema sit amen', an ex libris
                        inscription no doubt in the usual s. xiii St Albans form. 'H' is given in
                        green (as in Laud Misc. 370), the rest, as usual, in red.</p><p>Pledged in
                        1453(?) in cista regine for 33s 4d by M. John Consobrinus (alias Portigall)
                        and Dom. John Warder, and again by the same at intervals between 1455 and
                        1462(?). Fr. John Consobrinus was a Carmelite, who died from drinking a
                        poisonous potion in Lisbon in 1475; he incepted as DD at Oxford c. 1449. His
                        Benedictine friend John Wardale, who was subcellarer of St Albans in 1452,
                        supplicated for the BD in 1454.</p><p>The stationer's monogram IC and price
                        mark xxxiii s. iiij d. is on fol. iii above the cauciones. At end is a
                        caucio of 1467.</p></provenance>
               </history>
